{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"A0A2I2F2J6","uniProtkbId":"CFOK_ASPCN","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"2024-01-24","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2026-09-02","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"2018-02-28","entryVersion":17,"sequenceVersion":1},"annotationScore":3.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Aspergillus candidus","taxonId":41067,"lineage":["Eukaryota","Fungi","Dikarya","Ascomycota","Pezizomycotina","Eurotiomycetes","Eurotiomycetidae","Eurotiales","Aspergillaceae","Aspergillus","Circumdati"]},"proteinExistence":"1: Evidence at protein level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"36704842"}],"value":"Chalcone isomerase cfoK"},"shortNames":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"36704842"}],"value":"CHI cfoK"}],"ecNumbers":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36704842"}],"value":"5.5.1.6"}]},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"36704842"}],"value":"Chlorflavonin biosynthesis cluster protein K"}}]},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"36704842"}],"value":"cfok"},"orfNames":[{"value":"BDW47DRAFT_111389"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36704842"}],"value":"Chalcone isomerase; part of the gene cluster that mediates the biosynthesis of chlorflavonin, a fungal flavonoid with acetolactate synthase inhibitory activity (PubMed:36704842). Within the pathway, cfoK acts as chalcone isomerase (CHI), the key enzyme responsible for the tricyclic formation of flavanone through Michael-type intramolecular cyclization of chalcone. The hydrogen at C2'-OH is extracted by the imidazole ring of His-33, which induces the oxa-Michael addition to form the intermediate enolate through 6-endo-trig mode cyclization. The enolate can then be stabilized by a hydrogen bond with the Tyr-50 residue. Following enol tautomerization, the C ring, a gamma-pyranone ring, is formed (PubMed:36704842). The pathway begins with the PKS-NRPS hybrid synthetase cfoA that uses benzoic acid or p-hydroxybenzoic acid as a starter unit with four rounds of chain elongation using malonyl-CoA to form the chalcone skeleton. Then, a new type of chalcone isomerase, cfoK, catalyzes the conversion of the chalcone into a flavanone by a histidine-mediated oxa-Michael addition mechanism. The desaturation of flavanone to flavone is catalyzed by a new type of flavone synthase, the flavin mononucleotide (FMN)-dependent oxidoreductase cfoJ. Monooxygenases cfoF, cfoG, and P450 cfoH are responsible for the hydroxylation of the flavonoid skeleton at sites C3, C8, and C2', respectively. Like cfoF, the dehydratase cfoI plays also a role in the hydroxylation of position C3. Methyltransferases cfoB, cfoC, and cfoD then catalyze the methylation of C7-OH, C8-OH, and C3-OH, respectively. Finally, the monooxygenase cfoE is responsible for the chlorination of flavonoid at position C3' (PubMed:36704842)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"commentType":"CATALYTIC ACTIVITY","reaction":{"name":"a chalcone = a flavanone.","ecNumber":"5.5.1.6","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36704842"}]}},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36704842"}],"value":"Secondary metabolite biosynthesis; flavonoid biosynthesis"}],"commentType":"PATHWAY"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"36704842"}],"value":"Impairs the formation of flavonones and leads to the accumulation of chalcone intermediates"}],"commentType":"DISRUPTION PHENOTYPE"}],"features":[{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":254,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Chalcone isomerase cfoK","featureId":"PRO_0000459542"},{"type":"Active site","location":{"start":{"value":33,"modifi